WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.290
The Membrane. A fail-closed
authorization gate for AI agents.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:10.433
An agent with production access is about
to act.

00:00:10.483 --> 00:00:15.384
Before it can, the gate issues one
thing: a signed scope, valid for

00:00:15.384 --> 00:00:19.649
fifteen minutes, naming the model and
the tools it may use.

00:00:19.699 --> 00:00:22.150
Nothing outside that scope is permitted.

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:26.270
The agent comments on a Jira ticket.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:29.783
That's inside its scope, so the call is
allowed, and the

00:00:29.783 --> 00:00:32.949
gate writes a receipt, chained to the
one before it.

00:00:32.999 --> 00:00:35.610
Tool effects in this recording are
simulated.

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:40.194
Now the agent tries to merge on GitHub.

00:00:40.244 --> 00:00:42.711
That tool is not in its scope.

00:00:42.761 --> 00:00:45.576
The call is blocked before it reaches
anything, and

00:00:45.576 --> 00:00:47.900
the denial is recorded as a signed
receipt.

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:53.053
It tries again, this time with a
different model.

00:00:53.103 --> 00:00:55.420
Blocked again, and recorded.

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:59.850
The operator replays the audit trail.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:04.963
The scope issued, one allowed call, two
denials.

00:01:05.013 --> 00:01:08.828
Each event carries its hash and its
reason.

00:01:08.878 --> 00:01:12.516
Then the chain is verified: every
receipt hash

00:01:12.516 --> 00:01:15.550
is recomputed, every signature checked.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:19.059
Every receipt checks out.

00:01:19.109 --> 00:01:23.400
The gate fails closed, and the evidence
is yours to keep.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:30.623
The Membrane. Self-hosted, fail-closed
authorization for AI agents.

00:01:30.673 --> 00:01:33.480
membrane.dojopop.live
